        "heading": "The buyer situation: a denier gets picked before the cloth",
        "body": "A weaving program normally begins with a fabric specification, not a yarn order. It names a target weight, a hand, a drape and an end use. That end use might be a lightweight scarf, a structured jacket or a lined dress. To source the yarn, the buyer opens a raw silk spec sheet. On the [downloadable raw silk spec sheet](/raw-silk-spec-sheet.md) sits one short denier row: 20/22D is standard, and 13/19D is also in regular supply and 27/29D is available on request. The full row is also published on the [raw silk specs page](https://www.christopherbu.com/raw-silk-specs), 2026.\n\nBecause the row resembles a menu, buyers treat it as one. A smaller number appears delicate and goes to a lightweight fabric. A bigger number appears strong and goes to a heavy one. The denier is fixed, the order proceeds, and the fabric is woven. Only then does the mismatch appear: the trial piece returns too stiff, too thin, or hanging with the wrong drape.\n\nFor example, a buyer planning a crisp, lightweight blouse may order 27/29D. The assumption is that a thicker yarn will be more durable. The resulting fabric comes back heavy and boardy. That forces the sett to be reopened and the trial discarded, delaying the program. The denier was never the problem; choosing it first was.\n\nA quieter version appears when a buyer reads 27/29D as a superior grade and pays a premium. The yarn runs no cleaner on the loom, because grade and denier are separate measurements. Consider how rarely a spec sheet is read top to bottom in one sitting. The commercial lines get read, while the construction logic behind them does not."
