        "body": "Denier does not stay in the yarn. It moves into the cloth through a handful of linked effects. These effects are qualitative and hold at a fixed sett and weave.\n\n**Sett** is the number of warp ends and weft picks per unit of width. Keep the sett fixed and a heavier filament fills more of the space between threads. The fabric gains cover and weight, feels fuller, and hangs with more body. A finer filament leaves more air in the same sett. That fabric tends to be lighter, softer and more sheer.\n\n**Cover** is how fully the yarns hide the space between them. A coarser denier tends to raise cover at a given sett. **Hand** is the way the fabric feels when you hold it. A heavier filament tends to give a firmer hand, a finer one a softer hand. Drape follows the same line. Lightweight fabric tends to fall in soft folds, while heavy fabric tends to hold a shape.\n\nSurface and colour also respond. A finer, more even yarn can read as smoother and a little brighter. A coarser yarn shows more texture. Dye uptake changes with yarn size and twist, so a shade matched on one denier may not read the same on another. All products here are available greige (undyed) or dyed to order from a standard shade card or a custom colour ([product range](/products/)). Finish is therefore a separate choice from fineness.\n\nNone of these effects is a defect; they are consequences. The mistake is expecting a denier swap to leave the fabric unchanged. It is also expecting a bigger number to repair a quality problem, which it will not.",
