        "heading": "How to run the checklist before you travel",
        "body": "Use one rule: a row closes only when the document named in it exists, and the moment named in it has passed. Ask two questions of each row: what paper satisfies this check, and when must that paper exist. A field with no document and no date is a promise, and the buyer should leave that row open. Write the answers down, one line per field.\n\nRun the method in order. Start with the 5 groups of the written quotation. For each field, name the document that satisfies it and the moment it must exist. Then verify each certificate number through the OEKO-TEX label check and the GOTS public licence database. Record the result. Then check California Prop 65 and EU textile safety against the product-specific test reports. Finally, confirm destination-market compliance in writing before order approval. Work down the table once, in order, and mark each row open or closed.\n\nThe methodology is plain: it turns a pile of claims into a page of open and closed rows. Keep the [downloadable raw silk spec sheet](/raw-silk-spec-sheet.md) beside the quotation as you work. Raise any field whose evidence is not obvious with the seller in writing. A field that needs a phone call to explain is a field that should be stated on paper.\n\nBefore you approve any raw silk order, [request a written quotation](/contact/) and work down the checklist on paper. Raise every open row with the seller in writing before the visit. A quotation that survives all 5 groups is a document a buyer can sign. The audit day can then stay on the floor. Those rows are already closed. Ask for paper first."
