        "heading": "One judgement method: turn three lines into three process checks",
        "body": "Use one test on the three fields. For each metric, name the process stage it governs, then ask which document records the result for the lot.\n\nThe methodology is plain. Take a line that states evenness. The stage is warp regularity under tension. The document is the per-lot grading certificate, tied to the lot mark, with the test method reference named. Repeat for cleanness and neatness, and the three lines become three written questions instead of one vague request for better quality. A metric with a stage, a result and a document is checkable. A metric without them is a word on a page.\n\nFor instance, a buyer comparing two offers can put the three metrics in a short table with two extra columns: the stage each constrains, and the document that proves it. Whichever offer names all three stages and supplies the certificate reference can be accepted against a specific shipment. It also gives the buyer three questions to send in writing instead of one request for a higher grade.\n\nDenier, variety and finish are separate rows in the same framework. The [product range](https://www.christopherbu.com/products/) lists four varieties: 20/22D white raw silk, natural dupion silk yarn, 1-ply luxury silk yarn, and GOTS-certified organic silk. [Trade and shipping terms](https://www.christopherbu.com/trade-terms/) cover the commercial fields. Read the three metrics beside those rows and anchor every one to a lot."
