    "intro": "A raw silk order should not be approved until the lot mark and the per-lot grading certificate are written down. That is the short answer. A grade such as 5A is a claim about one production lot. It is not a property of a product line. Without a lot mark, no document links the claim to specific yarn. Without a grading certificate for that lot, no test result supports the claim. Both fields sit in the written quotation acceptance framework. Both have to exist on paper before a buyer signs.\n\nConsider a first bulk order. A buyer checks 20/22D, 5A and the unit price, then approves. Weeks later a weaving run shows uneven yarn. The buyer asks which lot the grade claim covered. The lot mark and the per-lot certificate were on the quotation. Nobody read them as a test. The paperwork was there. The check was not.",
