        "body": "A lot mark lets a buyer follow cloth backwards. The site publishes a worked example.\n\nThe site states that the mulberry silk woven in Yizhou is the base of the couture gowns Christopher Bu designs. It names three garments: the dupioni cut for Fan Bingbing in 2013, the jacquard Forbidden City gown she wore to the 2015 Met Gala, and the Snow Plum Blossom dress Paz Vega wore to the 2018 Oscars. Tagged as a first-party record, \"the label's own production records place the silk for these gowns in Yizhou\" (Christopher Bu, Yizhou mulberry silk archive, 2026). The 2013 pieces are \"cut from the same dupioni we supply as greige today.\" The site names these gowns specifically; it does not say every gown or season used this silk.\n\nThe spec link is concrete. The spec sheet lists 20/22D as standard denier, natural dupion silk yarn among four varieties and greige (undyed) as a finish on all products (Christopher Bu, raw silk spec sheet, 2026). Local mills reel 20/22 denier raw silk graded 4A to 6A under GB/T 1797-2008 (Standardization Administration of China, 2008), and the site's Yizhou article says a jacquard cannot make a clean motif from uneven yarn. So the evenness a buyer checks per lot, tied by the lot mark / traceability field, is the property that let the Forbidden City jacquard carry its woven motif. The greige dupioni a buyer orders today is the cloth the site names behind the 2013 pieces. That is the audit trail lot-level traceability buys: a finished gown back to a grade, a lot and a Yizhou origin.\n\nKeep the boundary plain. The records name an origin and a cloth, not a lot number, a certificate number or a formal traceability scheme. The about page describes sourcing raw silk, greige fabric and dupioni from local mills, with Yizhou as the geographic origin. One 2013 piece called \"stamp-printed\" is a finished archive garment, not a service offered to buyers.",
