        "heading": "What grade consistency looks like in a finished cloth",
        "body": "A grade tier describes yarn at the reeling stage. The clearest way to see why that matters is a finished cloth with a large, uninterrupted lustrous surface. The site says Yizhou raw silk regularly reaches 5A and 6A, the highest quality tiers, on the [raw silk spec page](https://www.christopherbu.com/raw-silk-specs/). That is a statement about yarn from one geographic origin, not a claim about any single gown. The site's editorial says the connection runs through \"three different crafts but one cloth\" (Christopher Bu site editorial, 2026). It states the buyer's point plainly: \"A buyer who wants that consistency is really asking for the same origin, not the same style\". The same editorial labels the provenance as a first-party record and notes that the label's own production records place the silk for these gowns in Yizhou.\n\nThe Forbidden City jacquard gown Fan Bingbing wore to the 2015 Met Gala makes the link concrete. Its palace motif is woven into the cloth on the loom, so the pattern sits inside the weave, and the gown presents a large, uninterrupted lustrous surface where uneven yarn has nowhere to hide. The site notes that a jacquard loom cannot make a clean motif from uneven yarn. On the [downloadable spec sheet](https://www.christopherbu.com/raw-silk-spec-sheet.md), the per-batch quality evidence group records evenness, cleanness and neatness, grade and quality tier by lot, a lot mark, and a grading certificate per lot. The evenness and cleanness a buyer checks on paper are the same qualities that let a loom lay a clean palace motif across a broad silk face. The grade tier and the lot mark tie those readings to one batch. Those fields are exactly the ones a written quotation has to state or confirm before a buyer accepts a shipment. The site names three gowns from this one silk base: the dupioni cut for Fan Bingbing in 2013, the jacquard of the Forbidden City gown, and the Snow Plum Blossom dress Paz Vega wore to the 2018 Oscars. Dupioni, jacquard and embroidery all begin from the same reeling and grading, then diverge at the design.\n\nTwo limits apply. A grade tier by itself does not predict how a finished garment looks, moves or drapes. The design, the weave and the work added afterwards shape the finished result. On the Forbidden City gown, embroidery was applied only after the jacquard came off the loom. Naming these gowns is not a claim that every gown, collection or runway season used this silk. What a buyer is really specifying, when the quotation asks for a grade and the spec sheet asks for evenness, is the same Yizhou origin recorded per lot from local mills. The grade records the yarn; it makes no promise about a finished dress.",
