    "intro": "A written raw silk quotation works as an acceptance framework. Most buyers read it as a price sheet. They look for denier, grade and price, find all three, and stop. The rest of the document gets filed as boilerplate. The fields that decide whether a shipment can be accepted sit further down, in the parts nobody reads on the first pass.\n\nThose fields name the legal seller, tie every grade claim to a lot, fix the test method behind an inspection report, and say when a certificate number becomes binding. When a buyer skips them, the problem shows up after the yarn reaches the loom. The site's own couture archive gives the reason: the same Yizhou reeling and grading produced three different cloths, a 2013 dupioni piece, the jacquard of a 2015 Met Gala gown and an embroidered 2018 Oscars dress. This article sets out what a written quotation must contain, why the acceptance framework gets missed, and one method for checking it. The aim is a document a buyer can use to accept or reject goods, not just to compare prices.",
