        "heading": "Why buyers miss the acceptance framework at first pass",
        "body": "One root cause explains the miss. A buyer opens a quotation to answer 'What will this cost?' The acceptance fields answer a different question: 'What will count as conforming goods?' Both questions sit on the same page. The cheaper one wins attention, so the second one never gets asked.\n\nConsider a first bulk order, for example. A buyer checks 20/22D, 5A and the unit price, then signs. The lot mark, the grading certificate per lot and the test method reference are still on the page. They carry no price tag, so they look like small print. Later, when a weaving run shows uneven yarn, the buyer asks which lot the grade claim covered. The answer was in the quotation all along. The buyer never read that part as a test.\n\nThis is a reading problem, not a missing data problem. The [raw silk spec page](/raw-silk-specs/) publishes the field list in full. The habit that fails is the habit of stopping at the commercial lines. The grading tiers behind those lines follow GB/T 1797-2008, listed on the [certificate check page](https://www.christopherbu.com/certificates/). A buyer who knows the standard exists still has to check that the quotation names it.\n\nTwo features make the miss predictable. Price, denier and grade sit near the top, so they get read. The check and compliance fields sit lower, spread across five groups, so they get skimmed. For a buyer comparing several suppliers in one afternoon, skimming is rational. It is also the moment the acceptance framework disappears.\n\nThe [20/22D white raw silk yarn](/products/raw-silk/) page shows the product side of the same fields: denier, grade and finish. That is exactly the trio a first pass tends to stop at. The quotation adds the rest, and the rest is what protects the buyer. Read the whole page."
