        "heading": "Why a written quotation has to name grade, denier, weave and lot",
        "body": "One origin does not produce one cloth. The site states that the mulberry silk woven in Yizhou is the base of the couture gowns Christopher Bu designs, and that dupioni, jacquard and embroidery all begin from the same reeling and grading. A buyer who writes \"Yizhou mulberry silk\" on an order has named a place and left the cloth unspecified. The written quotation has to go further: grade, denier, weave or fabric category, and lot. Those four fields are what separate one cloth from another.\n\nThe site's [Yizhou article](/blog/yizhou-mulberry-silk-longevity-to-world/) puts it directly. It says dupioni, jacquard and embroidery \"all begin from the same reeling and grading\". It adds that \"a buyer who wants that consistency is really asking for the same origin, not the same style\". The label's own production records place the silk for these gowns in Yizhou, tagged on the site as a first-party record. The site names the goods as raw silk, greige fabric and dupioni from local mills, with Yizhou as the geographic origin."
