        "heading": "The three cloths behind one origin",
        "body": "The archive names three garments, and each one is a different fabric decision. The dupioni cut for Fan Bingbing in 2013 came from the Yizhou silk base. So did the jacquard of the Forbidden City gown she wore to the 2015 Met Gala, and the Snow Plum Blossom dress Paz Vega wore to the 2018 Oscars. These are the only pieces the site names in that chain. The claim does not cover every gown, every collection or every season.\n\nArchive fact: the site describes a 2013 Christopher Bu dupioni skirt as stamp-printed and says the two 2013 pieces reappear on the label's own lookbook models, cut from the same dupioni the label supplies as greige today. Stamp-printing here describes a finished archive garment and is not a Christopher Bu B2B capability.\n\nArchive fact: the jacquard of the Forbidden City gown Fan Bingbing wore to the 2015 Met Gala came from the same Yizhou silk base as the 2013 dupioni pieces. The site names the Snow Plum Blossom dress Paz Vega wore to the 2018 Oscars in that same chain. It tags the Yizhou attribution to the label's own production records as a first-party record.\n\nArchive fact: Paz Vega wore the Christopher Bu Snow Plum Blossom dress to the 2018 Oscars. The site describes white mulberry silk with red plum blossoms applied by hand embroidery, one of the three gowns it names in the Yizhou chain.",
