        "heading": "What the gowns prove about the spec fields",
        "body": "The link between a spec field and a finished gown is concrete. The spec sheet fixes 20/22D as the standard denier and grades the yarn 4A, 5A or 6A under GB/T 1797-2008, with moisture controlled below 11% (Standardization Administration of China, 2008). The per-batch group then adds evenness, cleanness and neatness by lot. Those fields decide whether a clean woven motif is possible. The site's Yizhou article says a jacquard loom cannot make a clean motif from uneven yarn. Its Forbidden City article records that the palace motif was woven into the mulberry silk jacquard, with embroidery added to the finished gown afterwards in the Christopher Bu atelier. Evenness and cleanness, tied to a lot mark and a per-lot grading certificate, are the paper trail behind that clean woven motif. A quotation that states only \"5A\" does not say which cloth that grade was measured on.\n\nDenier picks the yarn, and grade sets the quality ceiling. The weave or fabric category decides whether the cloth is dupioni, jacquard or the greige base an embroiderer works on, while the lot mark ties that choice to one production lot. Strip any one of them and a buyer has an origin with no cloth attached. The 2013 dupioni pieces, the 2015 jacquard gown and the 2018 Snow Plum Blossom dress are the visible proof that one reeling and grading can end as three different fabrics on a body.\n\nAsk for grade, denier, weave or fabric category, and lot mark on the written quotation. The five-group field list carries grade and quality tier by lot, evenness, cleanness, neatness, the lot mark and the per-lot grading certificate. The spec sheet adds the denier and the fabric varieties. Work down those rows and mark each one open or closed before you approve the order.",
