        "heading": "Verify a certificate claim before you approve",
        "body": "A certificate claim on a quotation is a scope claim, and it needs a public check. The written quotation and shipping documents carry the certificate numbers, so a buyer can fact check each one against the issuing body before approval. The number alone proves nothing until its scope matches the goods.\n\nThe [certificate check page](/certificates/) lists two entry points. The [OEKO-TEX label check](https://www.oeko-tex.com/en/business/certification/management/label-check) lets a buyer confirm a label and its scope (OEKO-TEX Association, retrieved 2026-11-06). The [GOTS public licence database](https://certified.gots.org/) does the same for organic status (Global Organic Textile Standard, retrieved 2026-11-06). Raw silk grading follows GB/T 1797-2008 (Standardization Administration of China, 2008).\n\nDestination-market compliance works the same way. Buyers check California Prop 65 and EU textile safety requirements against product-specific test reports, and both sides confirm them in writing before order approval. That review is a field on the quotation, not a promise made after shipment. For example, a buyer shipping to the EU can ask which test report covers a given claim, then check the report against the certificate scope. One claim, one report, one written confirmation. If any of the three is missing, the field stays open.\n\nThe specific action is simple. Before you approve an order, [request a written quotation](/contact/) and read all five groups against the published field list. Ask for the field list on paper, then tick each row. The [sample request page](/samples/) covers the swatch that should arrive with the quotation. A quotation that survives all five groups is a document a buyer can sign.",
